WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.464
- You know my next guests
as the very funny doctors

00:00:02.464 --> 00:00:04.620
from the hit series Scrubs.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.334
Now they have a podcast called
Fake Doctors, Real Friends

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.408
where they break down the good, the bad

00:00:09.408 --> 00:00:11.500
and the hilariously embarrassing moments

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.865
of every single Scrubs episode,

00:00:12.865 --> 00:00:14.167
which we all wanna know about.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:17.576
I'm talking about Zach
Braff and Donald Faison.

00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:18.813
Hey what's up, y'all?

00:00:18.813 --> 00:00:20.160
- What is good?

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.521
- Thank you so much for doing this show,

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.770
I really, really appreciate it.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:24.950
I know it's an awkward thing,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.270
we tried to make it look like my show,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.103
and I love that--
(laughs)

00:00:28.103 --> 00:00:28.936
- Is that not a real background?

00:00:28.936 --> 00:00:30.074
Is that like a virtual background?

00:00:30.074 --> 00:00:32.900
- No dude, this is my former living room

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.850
and it's gone and they literally

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.478
put a whole set up behind me.

00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:37.859
- It looks so cool.

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:39.350
- Donald are you in a closet?

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.615
- I am in my closet.

00:00:40.615 --> 00:00:42.402
This is where I escape reality.

00:00:42.402 --> 00:00:45.450
I escape the wife, I escape the kids,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.546
and this is also where we do the podcast.

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:50.440
- That's amazing, it is funny
in this like, pandemic time

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.190
to find especially like we
were in a cabin in Montana,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.170
now I'm in LA but,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.800
we were in a cabin in
Montana, it's so small

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.100
and there's like nowhere to go,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.639
and like literally would
end up in a bathroom.

00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:00.264
(laughs)
And I was like, this is weird.

00:01:00.264 --> 00:01:01.574
This is weird,
- Yeah.

00:01:01.574 --> 00:01:02.720
- That I'm in a bathroom
- And like,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.640
we started recording the podcast

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.890
and Donald needed to find
a place that was like,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.160
good for sound, and so he
started with his closet.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.585
And then he realized
that his kids didn't want

00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:14.210
to come in there, and his
wife would leave him alone.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.920
And so now I think his whole family thinks

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.390
he makes the podcast like every day.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.276
- (laughs) He's just in
there doing crosswords

00:01:21.276 --> 00:01:22.944
- Absolutely.
- Absolutely.

00:01:22.944 --> 00:01:24.903
- Absolutely, sometimes I doodle.

00:01:24.903 --> 00:01:26.821
(all laugh)

00:01:26.821 --> 00:01:28.520
- Oh my god.
(Zach and Donald laughing)

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.820
Anyways, I just wanna ask you though,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.054
because y'all are actual,
like real friends.

00:01:32.054 --> 00:01:33.309
And that's why I like your podcast,

00:01:33.309 --> 00:01:36.190
like fake doctors you
played on the TV show,

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.023
but you're real friends.

00:01:37.023 --> 00:01:37.856
Like has it been hard?

00:01:37.856 --> 00:01:39.190
Like I know we're all
like with our family,

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:40.250
it's nice to have that family time.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.340
But I miss friends, like
do y'all miss each other,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.190
like hanging out?

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.076
- [Donald] Oh I miss him tremendously.

00:01:45.076 --> 00:01:46.010
- So much.
- I miss him so much, man.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:46.843
- That's what started, y'know,

00:01:46.843 --> 00:01:48.350
we were gonna do this
podcast before quarantine,

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.677
and 'cause we were planning on doing it.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.091
And then when quarantine happened,

00:01:52.091 --> 00:01:54.084
it became a thing where,

00:01:54.084 --> 00:01:55.928
Donald and I, we can't see each other.

00:01:55.928 --> 00:01:58.300
So like twice a week when we record,

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.992
we have like an hour
and a half to just laugh

00:02:00.992 --> 00:02:02.956
and tell stories and catch up.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.360
And y'know we talk about the show,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.410
we talk about episodes of the show,

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.076
that's sort of like the
structure of the show.

00:02:08.076 --> 00:02:10.000
But then we go off on all these tangents,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.789
and it's actually given
us a formal structure

00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:14.390
to like check in twice a week,

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.090
and tell stories and like crack up.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.770
And it's like therapy,
- Yeah.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.770
- Because y'know we're all lonesome,

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.122
and we're just cracking up.

00:02:21.122 --> 00:02:22.970
- Yeah no, I--
- Yeah it's great,

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.512
it's great to be able to
talk to your best friend

00:02:24.512 --> 00:02:27.540
for an hour and a half away
from the kids in silence,

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.203
for y'know, twice a week.

00:02:29.203 --> 00:02:30.036
It's wonderful.

00:02:30.036 --> 00:02:31.760
- Okay but just speaking up for your wife,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.320
know that she wants that hour
to talk to her girls, okay.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.140
- Oh no I know, trust me I know.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.560
She complains about it all the time,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.029
she's like "you know
I wish I had a podcast

00:02:39.029 --> 00:02:39.862
where I could just--"

00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:40.695
- I tell her,

00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:41.707
- [Kelly] I wish I had
a reason to walk out--

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:43.990
- By the way I told CaCee, who's y'know,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.440
Jessica Simpson's her best friend.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.286
I go "Why don't you do a
podcast with Jessica Simpson?

00:02:47.286 --> 00:02:48.577
You can have your own time."

00:02:48.577 --> 00:02:49.697
- And she's like

00:02:49.697 --> 00:02:50.530
"I should do that"
- First of all,

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.944
How funny would that be?
- That'd be hilarious!

00:02:52.944 --> 00:02:54.200
- I know, I know.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.250
- Wait--
- And then y'all could like,

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.864
you could merge yours sometimes.

00:02:56.864 --> 00:02:58.200
- Right. (laughs)
- It could all come together.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.852
- Well okay so, frontline workers

00:03:00.852 --> 00:03:02.900
are really saving us these days.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.761
And this is something
I actually never knew,

00:03:04.761 --> 00:03:07.293
y'all actually shot
that at a real hospital.

00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:08.126
- [Zach] Yeah.

00:03:08.126 --> 00:03:09.527
- I was thinking that
was a set the whole time.

00:03:09.527 --> 00:03:10.810
- [Donald] No.
- No we took over

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.354
an enormous hospital,

00:03:12.354 --> 00:03:14.170
and we couldn't believe it either.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.340
And everything was built into it.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.173
There was four floors,

00:03:17.173 --> 00:03:19.290
well then there was the
basement, and the roof,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.017
so technically six,
'cause we used all of it.

00:03:21.017 --> 00:03:24.680
And all our dressing
rooms were on one floor.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.626
All the production offices
were another floor.

00:03:26.626 --> 00:03:30.230
And then we would film every
single corner of the place.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.840
Even the sets like our apartment,

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.130
that weren't the hospital,

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.860
they built into the hospital.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:35.740
Kind of like your set

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.008
that's built into your
living room you said.

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:38.466
So--
- That's what's happening.

00:03:38.466 --> 00:03:40.680
- Yeah, so it was amazing.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.005
We kinda had our own campus.

00:03:42.005 --> 00:03:44.570
We lived in an abandoned hospital

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.386
for eight and a half years.

00:03:46.386 --> 00:03:48.450
- Yeah, it was really weird though.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.380
For the first couple of seasons y'know,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.220
I guess the hospital had just
recently become abandoned,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.868
and people would just
straggle in off the street.

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.448
- Yep.
- Asking for, y'know,

00:03:56.448 --> 00:03:58.365
asking for medicine and stuff.

00:03:58.365 --> 00:03:59.570
- You're dressed up like doctors!

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:00.917
- Yeah.
- No and we'd be like,

00:04:00.917 --> 00:04:01.861
(laughs)
"Sorry this isn't

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:02.830
a real hospital."

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.220
And they'd look around and see everyone

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.849
in costume in scrubs, in
nurses' outfits and everything,

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:08.913
and be like "What are you talking about?"

00:04:08.913 --> 00:04:10.292
(laughs)
- Oh my god.

00:04:10.292 --> 00:04:12.065
- I need your assistance now!

00:04:12.065 --> 00:04:14.510
- At the entrance they
had to put up a huge sign

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.770
like "This is not a working hospital.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.321
Do not come here for emergency care."

00:04:18.321 --> 00:04:19.203
- Yeah.

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:22.570
- Wow, and I would have
felt horrible about that.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.663
Like, turning people away.

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:26.320
- Yeah we had to be like "A
couple of miles down that way."

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.869
- You're like "Trust me, you don't want me

00:04:27.869 --> 00:04:30.380
to do anything." (laughs)
- [Donald] Oh, no--

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.280
- Okay so Donald, since
you've been isolated

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.330
from each other for a while,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.385
what do you miss doing the most together?

00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:37.835
- I miss dinners, y'know what I mean.

00:04:37.835 --> 00:04:39.950
'Cause we get a chance to talk a lot.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.042
I miss dinners and drinking wine together,

00:04:42.042 --> 00:04:44.766
and y'know, laughing over a meal.

00:04:44.766 --> 00:04:46.514
I miss that the most.

00:04:46.514 --> 00:04:48.843
- Okay all of a sudden
I wanna be real friends

00:04:48.843 --> 00:04:49.676
with you as well.

00:04:49.676 --> 00:04:50.509
(laughs)
- Because--

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:51.356
- Will you come be real friends with us?

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:52.695
(laughs)
- You're more than welcome to.

00:04:52.695 --> 00:04:53.540
- I mean that sounds awesome.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:55.170
- Kelly really the only
reason Donald and I

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:56.410
wanted to come on your show,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:57.762
other than to plug our podcast,

00:04:57.762 --> 00:04:59.468
was to hope that maybe
you would be our friend

00:04:59.468 --> 00:05:01.192
and come to dinner with
us when this is all over.

00:05:01.192 --> 00:05:03.580
(Kelly laughs)
- You are welcome any time.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.810
- I am like literally,

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.829
like I think we're all
so excited too about,

00:05:06.829 --> 00:05:09.810
I mean dinner parties, or
going out to restaurants.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.482
Anything like other than
what's been happening,

00:05:11.482 --> 00:05:13.280
like I am down.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.473
- I know.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:14.473 --> 00:05:15.306
- So Zach have you decided--

00:05:15.306 --> 00:05:16.605
- It's so nice to see new faces.

00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:17.703
You know when you--
- I know.

00:05:17.703 --> 00:05:19.128
- When you get on a Zoom call,

00:05:19.128 --> 00:05:20.500
even if it's like for,

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:21.531
I don't know if you guys feel this,

00:05:21.531 --> 00:05:22.364
but when you get on a Zoom call,

00:05:22.364 --> 00:05:23.590
even if it's for work or something,

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:24.560
and you see new people,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.393
you're like,
- [Kelly] I know--

00:05:25.393 --> 00:05:26.226
- "tell me about your living room."--

00:05:26.226 --> 00:05:28.498
(Donald laughing)

00:05:28.498 --> 00:05:30.404
- And then you start telling stories

00:05:30.404 --> 00:05:31.971
that are really not good stories

00:05:31.971 --> 00:05:33.290
but it's just what's happening,

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.380
- [Zach] Yeah.
- and they're y'know,

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.360
so entertained by them.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.640
And I'm like why am I entertained by this,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.530
it's another level of sad.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.650
- And do you find yourself
looking at peoples' backgrounds,

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:41.577
Like, I'm like looking at like,

00:05:41.577 --> 00:05:42.410
"Oh what book is that?"
(laughs)

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.744
"Oh that's a bold paint
color, I might try that."

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:46.353
- Oh she got a--
- That actually happened to me

00:05:46.353 --> 00:05:48.670
when I was getting
interviewed by Seth Myers.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.400
And I actually recognized
one of the books,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.540
not realizing that he was
putting it up there to be funny,

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:53.720
that they're dumb books to him,

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.404
and I was like "Oh I've seen
that little miniseries."

00:05:55.404 --> 00:05:57.320
And he was like "Oh, yeah."

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:58.650
And then I just pissed myself.
(laughs)

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.913
Like, okay.

00:05:59.913 --> 00:06:01.293
What were you going to say, Donald?

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:03.400
- I was gonna say "Oh that's
a Gibson on the wall."

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.660
That was the first thing I recognized,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:05.493
- Oh!
- I was like,

00:06:05.493 --> 00:06:06.326
oh she got guitars on the wall!

00:06:06.326 --> 00:06:07.159
Is one of them a Gibson?

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:08.320
- Yeah both of them are Gibsons.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.920
- Oh, ooh. (laughs)
- Yes, yass.

00:06:12.256 --> 00:06:15.010
I play guitar poorly, that's
the best part of my show,

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.687
that we have guitars on
walls, but I play poorly.

